<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thukela River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tugela River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thukela, or Tugela, is the largest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a>, plunges over the Tugela Falls and flows about 500 kilometres east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. In the nineteenth century it was the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a> and the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colony-of-natal">Colony of Natal</a>, and it saw heavy fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thukela-river.jpg" alt="Thukela River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Bothar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphitheatre_Drakensberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
